<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Alcáçovas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1479">1479</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Alcáçovas-Toledo · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Alcáçovas, signed on 4 September 1479, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal. Afonso V of Portugal gave up his claim and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-la-beltraneja">Joanna la Beltraneja</a>, recognising Isabella I as queen of Castile. Castile kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, while Portugal kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> and the African trade south of the Canaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas.jpg" alt="Treaty of Alcáçovas" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public document conserved in the Archivo General de Simancas (Spain). Signature R.G.S. III-1480 302. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alca%C3%A7ovas1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
